Z||000000||FIEROCIOUS||05-18-2000||12:20 AM||||I've been thinking about polishing my intake manifold while I have the engine apart (super 2.8 project). <BR>Has anyone done this? <BR>Does it polish up well?<BR>How do you do it? (process ect.) ||||reg||1
Z||000001||GT Bastard||05-18-2000||12:27 AM||||I'm gonna do mine really soon. It's already off the car. I'll let you know if it registers high on the @ssometer.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000002||FIEROCIOUS||05-18-2000||02:35 AM||||By polish I mean the outside. For looks.||||reg||12
Z||000003||Standard||05-18-2000||03:49 AM||||I'm thinkin' about doing that.. my paints not quite the best, and I've really been gettin' into the polished aluminum thing lately..<P>It's just painted, so it shouldn't be hard to strip it and polish it up.. 300 -&gt; 600 -&gt; 800 -&gt; 1000 -&gt; 2000 grit paper, then alot of rubbing compound works really good.  I'm using that for the fork legs on my mountain bike.. can you say shiny?  The little crevices on the plenum would be tough, but I know there's a buffing/polishing kit for dremels that should work great.  I just have to find it... I think it would look sweet.  <p>[This message has been edited by Standard (edited 05-18-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||05-18-2000||09:13 AM||||use some paint stripper on it and go..... A variable speed dremmel and or vs die grinder will make short work of it. You'll get more choices of polishes, wheels, stones, sand papers, etc. with the die grinder which takes 1/4 inch shank bits. The selsction of things for Dremmel is limmited at best for most people.<P>Even if you don't fully polish it, cleaning up casting flash and other minor things like that and repainting it will make a tremendous improvement. Semitransparent candy paints always look good on a good clean aluminum part. <P>It's expensive, but you could also have it anodized. Anodizing is not like paint or powder coat which are clinging to the metal. The anodize finish literaly becomes part of the metal. You can scrape it up if something hard hits it but otherwise it's never going to chip flake or peal. Even if you ding it up it won't flake from the edges of the ding.||||reg||1
Z||000005||SCCA FIERO||05-18-2000||02:53 PM||||I have started this and it is a lost cause. The more you sand, the worse it gets. It seems there are alot of air bubbles trapped in the cast and they become exposed as you try to smooth it out. I can show you the one I have and you can see what I mean. I think the best thing to do is get it plated.||||reg||1
Z||000006||theogre||05-18-2000||06:53 PM||||nasty. low grade casting jobs. gotta love those.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Brian||05-18-2000||09:21 PM||||Not the best of pics but here's the result <A HREF="http://members.home.net/bmcf/Fiero/Images/Plenum300.jpg" TARGET=_blank>http://members.home.net/bmcf/Fiero/Images/Plenum300.jpg</A> <P>SCCA FIERO is correct. It's a real pain & you will never get all the bubbles out of the casting. Still, it shines up pretty good.<BR>Another down side is that you have to be very, very carefull while working in the engine compartment. A stray wrench or screwdriver will easily scratch the soft polished aluminium.||||reg||12
Z||000008||FIEROCIOUS||05-19-2000||01:35 AM||||Thanks all, SCCA that's what I thought when I looked at it I love this forum! thanks again Cliff. That does look good Brian! Ogre, I thought about candy colors also I may just do it. Thanks again!<BR>||||reg||1
